#20 0.144  --concurrency-range <start:end:step>: Determines the range of concurrency
#20 0.144 	 levels covered by the perf_analyzer. The perf_analyzer will start from
#20 0.144 	 the concurrency level of 'start' and go till 'end' with a stride of
#20 0.144 	 'step'. The default value of 'end' and 'step' are 1. If 'end' is not
#20 0.144 	 specified then perf_analyzer will run for a single concurrency
#20 0.144 	 level determined by 'start'. If 'end' is set as 0, then the concurrency
#20 0.144 	 limit will be incremented by 'step' till latency threshold is met.
#20 0.144 	 'end' and --latency-threshold can not be both 0 simultaneously. 'end'
#20 0.144 	 can not be 0 for sequence models while using asynchronous mode.
#20 0.144 --periodic-concurrency-range <start:end:step>: Determines the range of
#20 0.144 	 concurrency levels in the similar but slightly different manner as the
#20 0.144 	 --concurrency-range. Perf Analyzer will start from the concurrency level
#20 0.144 	 of 'start' and increase by 'step' each time. Unlike
#20 0.144 	 --concurrency-range, the 'end' indicates the *total* number of concurrency since
#20 0.144 	 the 'start' (including) and will stop increasing once the cumulative
#20 0.144 	 number of concurrent requests has reached the 'end'. The user can
#20 0.144 	 specify *when* to periodically increase the concurrency level using the
#20 0.144 	 --request-period option. The concurrency level will periodically
#20 0.144 	 increase for every n-th response specified by --request-period. Since
#20 0.144 	 this disables stability check in Perf Analyzer and reports response
#20 0.144 	 timestamps only, the user must provide --profile-export-file to
#20 0.144 	 specify where to dump all the measured timestamps. The default values of
#20 0.144 	 'start', 'end', and 'step' are 1.
#20 0.144 --request-period <n>: Indicates the number of responses that each request must
#20 0.144 	 receive before new, concurrent requests are sent when
#20 0.144 	 --periodic-concurrency-range is specified. Default value is 10.
#20 0.144 --request-parameter <name:value:type>: Specifies a custom parameter that can
#20 0.144 	 be sent to a Triton backend as part of the request. For example,
#20 0.144 	 providing '--request-parameter max_tokens:256:int' to the command line
#20 0.144 	 will set an additional parameter 'max_tokens' of type 'int' to 256 as
#20 0.144 	 part of the request. The --request-parameter may be specified
#20 0.144 	 multiple times for different custom parameters.
#20 0.144  --request-rate-range <start:end:step>: Determines the range of request rates
#20 0.144 	 for load generated by analyzer. This option can take floating-point
#20 0.144 	 values. The search along the request rate range is enabled only when
#20 0.144 	 using this option. If not specified, then analyzer will search
#20 0.144 	 along the concurrency-range. The perf_analyzer will start from the
#20 0.144 	 request rate of 'start' and go till 'end' with a stride of 'step'. The
#20 0.144 	 default values of 'start', 'end' and 'step' are all 1.0. If 'end' is
#20 0.144 	 not specified then perf_analyzer will run for a single request rate
#20 0.144 	 as determined by 'start'. If 'end' is set as 0.0, then the request
#20 0.144 	 rate will be incremented by 'step' till latency threshold is met.
#20 0.144 	 'end' and --latency-threshold can not be both 0 simultaneously.
#20 0.144  --request-distribution <"poisson"|"constant">: Specifies the time interval
#20 0.144 	 distribution between dispatching inference requests to the server.
#20 0.144 	 Poisson distribution closely mimics the real-world work load on a
#20 0.144 	 server. This option is ignored if not using --request-rate-range. By
#20 0.144 	 default, this option is set to be constant.
#20 0.144  --request-intervals: Specifies a path to a file containing time intervals in
#20 0.144 	 microseconds. Each time interval should be in a new line. The
#20 0.144 	 analyzer will try to maintain time intervals between successive generated
#20 0.144 	 requests to be as close as possible in this file. This option can be
#20 0.144 	 used to apply custom load to server with a certain pattern of
#20 0.144 	 interest. The analyzer will loop around the file if the duration of
#20 0.144 	 execution exceeds to that accounted for by the intervals. This option can
#20 0.144 	 not be used with --request-rate-range or --concurrency-range.
#20 0.144 --binary-search: Enables the binary search on the specified search range. This
#20 0.144 	 option requires 'start' and 'end' to be expilicitly specified in
#20 0.144 	 the --concurrency-range or --request-rate-range. When using this
#20 0.144 	 option, 'step' is more like the precision. Lower the 'step', more the
#20 0.144 	 number of iterations along the search path to find suitable
#20 0.144 	 convergence. By default, linear search is used.
#20 0.144 --num-of-sequences: Sets the number of concurrent sequences for sequence
#20 0.144 	 models. This option is ignored when --request-rate-range is not
#20 0.144 	 specified. By default, its value is 4.
#20 0.144  --latency-threshold (-l): Sets the limit on the observed latency. Analyzer
#20 0.144 	 will terminate the concurrency search once the measured latency
#20 0.144 	 exceeds this threshold. By default, latency threshold is set 0 and the
#20 0.144 	 perf_analyzer will run for entire --concurrency-range.
#20 0.144  --max-threads: Sets the maximum number of threads that will be created for
#20 0.144 	 providing desired concurrency or request rate. However, when runningin
#20 0.144 	 synchronous mode with concurrency-range having explicit 'end'
#20 0.144 	 specification,this value will be ignored. Default is 4 if
#20 0.144 	 --request-rate-range is specified otherwise default is 16.
#20 0.144  --stability-percentage (-s): Indicates the allowed variation in latency
#20 0.144 	 measurements when determining if a result is stable. The measurement is
#20 0.144 	 considered as stable if the ratio of max / min from the recent 3
#20 0.144 	 measurements is within (stability percentage)% in terms of both infer
#20 0.144 	 per second and latency. Default is 10(%).
#20 0.144  --max-trials (-r): Indicates the maximum number of measurements for each
#20 0.144 	 concurrency level visited during search. The perf analyzer will take
#20 0.144 	 multiple measurements and report the measurement until it is stable.
#20 0.144 	 The perf analyzer will abort if the measurement is still unstable
#20 0.144 	 after the maximum number of measurements. The default value is 10.
#20 0.144  --percentile: Indicates the confidence value as a percentile that will be
#20 0.144 	 used to determine if a measurement is stable. For example, a value of
